#import "style.typ": *
#chapter[What changed between Riemann and the announcement]

== The edge of the strip <c02s01>

Let $Psi(x)=sum_(p^k<=x) log p$. Giving a prime power weight $log p$ makes the derivative of $log zeta$ appear naturally. The prime number theorem is equivalent to $Psi(x) ∼ x$, here meaning that their ratio tends to one. To see the link with the usual form $pi(x) ∼ frac(x,log x)$, first remove higher powers. Their total weight is $O(sqrt(x)(log x)^2)$ by a direct count. Then apply summation by parts to the remaining sum of $log p$. This removes the slowly varying factor $log x$.

Hadamard and de la Vallée Poussin independently proved the prime number theorem in 1896. The decisive boundary was $Re s=1$. An instructive version of the zero-free-line argument uses the nonnegative trigonometric polynomial
$ 3+4cos theta+cos(2theta)=2(1+cos theta)^2. $
Multiply its value at $k t log p$ by $frac(p^(-k sigma),k)$ and sum over $p,k$, with $sigma>1$. Exponentiation gives
$ |zeta(sigma)|^3 |zeta(sigma+i t)|^4 |zeta(sigma+2i t)| >=1. $
If $t!=0$ and $zeta(1+i t)$ vanished, the middle factor would tend to zero to at least fourth order as $sigma$ decreased to one. The first would grow to only third order and the last would be bounded. This contradiction proves nonvanishing on that boundary. A Tauberian theorem or a suitable contour argument turns this analytic fact into the prime number theorem. This early proof already features a device that recurs in the modern argument. Positivity makes estimates available in one direction.

The prime number theorem leaves a wide range of possible error sizes. In 1901 von Koch connected Riemann's hypothesis to a square-root error, up to logarithmic factors. In modern notation RH yields $Psi(x)=x+O(sqrt(x)(log x)^2)$ and $pi(x)=op("Li")(x)+O(sqrt(x)log x)$. Conversely a bound $Psi(x)-x=O_(epsilon)(x^(frac(1,2)+epsilon))$ for every $epsilon>0$ excludes zeros to the right of the middle line. The latter implication follows by taking a transform of the error, as the next chapter will illustrate.

== The boundary value behind Dirichlet's progressions <c02s02>

Dirichlet's theorem can be read as an application of Euler's observation that the reciprocal prime sum diverges. To see the new ingredient, consider the odd residue classes modulo four. Write $chi(n)=1$ on $n equiv 1$ modulo four, $-1$ on $n equiv 3$ modulo four, and zero on even integers. The sums of $chi(n)$ over complete periods are zero. For real $s>=1$ the tail of $sum_n chi(n)n^(-s)$ after $N$ is $O(frac(1,N))$ uniformly for $s$ near one, by partial summation using those bounded partial sums. Thus the limit from $s>1$ agrees with the ordinary alternating series at one.

In this particular example the value is elementary. Integrating the finite geometric sum $1-t^2+t^4-dots+(-1)^m t^(2m)$ from zero to one gives the partial sum of $1-frac(1,3)+frac(1,5)-dots$. The integrated error against $frac(1,1+t^2)$ is bounded by $integral_0^1 t^(2m+2)dif t=frac(1,2m+3)$. Hence
$ L(1,chi)=integral_0^1 frac(dif t,1+t^2)=arctan(1)=frac(pi,4). $
The positivity and finiteness of this exact value allow a continuous real logarithm in a short interval to the right of one. The terms with exponent at least two in the logarithm of the Euler product are bounded there by a convergent series. Consequently $sum_p chi(p)p^(-s)$ stays bounded as $s$ decreases to one. Thus the *difference* between the two odd prime-class sums stays bounded, whereas their sum diverges like $log(frac(1,s-1))$ by Euler's product. Adding and subtracting shows that each progression receives one half of the divergent term. In particular each contains infinitely many primes.

For an arbitrary fixed modulus $q$, the orthogonality proved in Chapter 4 gives the class indicator on the units as an average of the characters. The analogous calculation yields, for $gcd(a,q)=1$ and real $s$ decreasing to one,
$ sum_(p equiv a " mod " q) p^(-s)=frac(1,varphi(q))log(frac(1,s-1))+O_(q)(1). $
The assumption used at the analytic step is that every nonprincipal character has $L(1,chi)!=0$. Then its Euler-product branch of the logarithm stays bounded on the real approach to one, since it agrees locally with a holomorphic logarithm up to a fixed multiple of $2pi i$. The principal character supplies the entire divergent term. Chapter 7 proves the necessary nonvanishing for all the characters including the difficult real case. The short calculation at modulus four demonstrates directly what that nonvanishing buys at the level of prime counts.

This conclusion is an assertion about a reciprocal-prime average. Dividing by $log(frac(1,s-1))$ and taking the limit gives the Dirichlet density of the class among all primes. The prime number theorem in arithmetic progressions is a stronger assertion about sharp counts up to $x$. It ultimately requires control on the whole boundary $Re s=1$, including nonzero imaginary parts. In the family problem further inside the strip, even a single proposed zero at an unknown height must be tested. Dirichlet's use of characters to distinguish progressions therefore leads naturally to the family of functions in the released proof. #link("https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~alexk/2022S572/Dirichlet1837.pdf")[Dirichlet's 1837 memoir.]

== Zeros on the line <c02s03>

Another approach works directly at $s=frac(1,2)+i t$ with $t$ real. The functional equation permits multiplication by a continuous factor of absolute value one to make the zeta values real on this line. A sign change of this real-valued function signals a zero. Hardy proved in 1914 that infinitely many zeros occur on the line. His result and the prime number theorem have distinct content. Nonvanishing on the edge constrains how far right the zeros can be. Producing infinitely many zeros on the middle line leaves open whether some other zero lies elsewhere.

How large a fraction of all zeros could be accounted for in this way? Let $N(T)$ count zeros in the strip with imaginary part between zero and $T$, with multiplicity, and let $N_(0)(T)$ count those on the middle line. Levinson's 1974 theorem put more than one third there in the limiting lower proportion. Conrey's 1989 work put at least two fifths there even when only simple zeros are counted in the numerator. Their methods use a Dirichlet polynomial, called a mollifier, to control mean values involving zeta. Even a ratio tending to one would permit an infinite exceptional set of relative density zero. The later argument in Part II has to detect a single proposed exception. #link("https://www.math.ntnu.no/emner/MA3001/2020v/2022v_MA3004/Levinson.pdf")[Levinson's paper.] #link("https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1989.399.1")[Conrey's paper.]

One can count all zeros up to a finite height using the argument principle and compare the count with certified zeros on the line. Platt and Trudgian verified the assertion up to height $3 dot 10^12$ using interval arithmetic. A bounded-height verification is a theorem about every zero in that bounded region. The analytical challenge is obtaining control at arbitrary height. In the proof studied here every proposed zero, however high, is tested by a Laplace transform at heights chosen in response to it.

== When a finite computation certifies every zero it counts <c02s04>

Computing on the line and certifying a height are two separate mathematical operations. On the critical line write $s=1/2+i t$. The completed zeta factor $pi^(-s/2)Gamma(s/2)zeta(s)$ is real there: its value at $s$ equals its value at $1-s=overline(s)$, which is the conjugate of its value at $s$. The gamma factor is finite and nonzero there, so one can remove its positive modulus and retain just its phase. With a continuous choice of argument along the line, this gives a real continuous function
$ Z(t)=e^(i vartheta(t))zeta(1/2+i t), quad
 vartheta(t)=Im log Gamma(1/4+i t/2)-frac(t,2)log pi. $
A different continuous branch changes the argument by an integral multiple of $2pi$ and hence leaves the exponential unchanged. The zeros of $Z$ on the real axis are precisely the zeta zeros on the central line at the indicated heights.

Suppose verified intervals for $Z(a)$ and $Z(b)$ lie strictly on opposite sides of zero. The intermediate value theorem gives a zero at some height between $a$ and $b$. Repeating this on disjoint open intervals supplies a rigorous lower bound for the number of distinct line zeros. An even-multiplicity zero can escape a sign-change test. A pair of simple zeros can also lie between successive sample points with equal signs at the endpoints. A floating-point value printed as exactly zero is a third, quite different situation: a rounding statement about one evaluation by itself gives no theorem about a neighborhood.

The missing ingredient is a count of *all* zeros, with multiplicity, in the portion of the strip under consideration. Suppose the boundary is zero-free and an independent count gives exactly nine. If nine disjoint intervals have opposite signs at their endpoints, every one contains exactly one simple zero, and there is no room for an off-line zero in this portion. If only seven such intervals have been found, two missing units of multiplicity could be explained by two additional real-line crossings, an even-multiplicity line zero, a higher multiplicity at an already located crossing, or an off-line pair reflected across the middle line. The precise interpretation of the count matters as much as the precision with which the real function was evaluated.

Turing's method, developed in his work with the Manchester computer, uses control of the integrated discrepancy in the zero count to certify completeness. In schematic terms, the argument principle gives an explicit smooth main term for the count together with an argument term. A zero missing from a list leaves a persistent positive shortfall in its counting staircase. Integration over a long enough range magnifies that shortfall. A proven bound on the integrated argument term makes it possible to test whether a proposed list is complete, with boundary and starting data incorporated. The interlude describes the argument-principle count, derives its smooth gamma-factor contribution, and states the classical bound on the remaining argument term. In the computation of Platt and Trudgian interval arithmetic controlled the signs and error bounds, yielding a theorem covering all zeros up to height $3 dot 10^12$. #link("https://doi.org/10.1112/plms/s3-3.1.99")[Turing's 1953 paper.] #link("https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.09765")[Platt and Trudgian.]

Why does completeness below a height, however large, leave a further question? Fix $T>0$, take $H>T$ and $0<a<1/2$, and set $z=s-1/2$. Consider
$ P(s)=(z^2-(a+i H)^2)(z^2-(a-i H)^2). $
This polynomial has real coefficients and is unchanged by $s mapsto 1-s$. Its four zeros are the off-line quartet $1/2 plus.minus a plus.minus i H$. Multiplying a completed function by $P$ preserves its two reflection symmetries and all its zeros through height $T$, including their multiplicities. The modified function of course has different values and a different Dirichlet-series problem. The example isolates the information in a finite zero table: exact agreement on that table and on both symmetries alone cannot determine the higher zeros. A proof using the Euler product or other arithmetic structure has substantially more information to work with. The construction in Part II responds to a *specified* zero at an arbitrary height. The real computation and the logical quantifier answer different historical questions.

== The arithmetic of a curve over a finite field <c02s05>

Let $C_0$ be a smooth projective geometrically connected curve of genus $g$ over the field of $q$ elements. Its zeta function records all the point counts $N_j=|C_(0)(𝔽_(q^j))|$,
$ cal("Z")(C_0,T)=exp(sum_(j>=1) frac(N_j T^j,j))
         = frac(product_(i=1)^(2g)(1-alpha_i T),(1-T)(1-q T)). $
The analogue of the middle-line assertion is $|alpha_i|=sqrt(q)$ for each $i$. Taking logarithms then gives $N_j=q^j+1-sum_i alpha_i^j$ and hence
$ |N_j-(q^j+1)|<=2g q^(frac(j,2)). $
For the projective line the genus is zero and the count is exactly $q^j+1$. In genus one there are two inverse roots, and a square-root bound on their sizes explains the sharp scale of the point-count error. Weil established the curve theorem. Deligne's 1974 work gave the corresponding assertion on weights in higher-dimensional smooth projective varieties.

Stepanov's method gives another view of the curve estimate. Construct an auxiliary function with forced zeros at rational points. If the function is nonzero and each forced zero has multiplicity at least $m$, the divisor of its poles bounds $m$ times the number of points. Linear algebra creates enough vanishing conditions while keeping a nonzero function. An effective proof has to balance imposed multiplicity against available degree. It is this role of multiplicity that reappears in the chain of theta lifts. The curve argument supplies a design principle. The integral divisibility needed over a number field requires a separate construction.

== Closed points and the weights of a finite-field curve <c02s06>

Let $C$ be a curve over $𝔽_q$ and work in an algebraic closure. The map $u mapsto u^q$ acts on the geometric points in finite orbits. A closed point of degree $d$ is an orbit of $d$ such points. Its points are defined over $𝔽_(q^n)$ exactly when $d$ divides $n$. If $b_d$ denotes the number of degree-$d$ closed points, then
$ N_n=sum_(d divides n) d b_d. $
This equality does not require the Riemann hypothesis for curves or an estimate. It is a fixed-point count in a union of cycles. Multiplying by $frac(T^n,n)$ and summing *as formal series* gives
$ sum_(n>=1) frac(N_n T^n,n)=sum_(d>=1) b_d sum_(k>=1) frac(T^(d k),k). $
Exponentiating gives $cal("Z")(C,T)=product_(d>=1)(1-T^d)^(-b_d)$. Each coefficient is determined by a finite amount of data, so this manipulation needs no analytic convergence. In the substitution $T=q^(-s)$ each local factor becomes the geometric series for the norm of a closed point. The product has exactly the arithmetic role of the product over primes.

For the affine line this product has a second elementary derivation. Its degree-$d$ closed points are the monic irreducible polynomials of degree $d$. Unique factorization makes the product the generating series of monic polynomials, and there are $q^n$ such polynomials of degree $n$. The result is $(1-q T)^(-1)$. Adding the point at infinity adds one closed point of degree one, multiplying the product by $(1-T)^(-1)$. Thus we recover the zeta function of the projective line without geometry beyond polynomial factorization. The poles at $T=1$ and $T=frac(1,q)$ have a common origin for all smooth projective geometrically connected curves, while the numerator records the contribution determined by the genus.

#example[A genus-one numerator computed at five][Consider $C: y^2=x^3-x$ over $𝔽_5$, with the point at infinity added. The roots $0,1,-1$ are distinct so the curve is nonsingular. For $x=0,1,2,3,4$ the right sides are respectively $0,0,1,4,0$, giving $1,1,2,2,1$ choices of $y$. Hence $N_1=8$. Using the elliptic-curve zeta theorem, write the numerator $1-a T+5T^2$. Comparison of the first coefficient gives $a=5+1-8=-2$. The reciprocal roots are $-1+2i$ and $-1-2i$, of absolute value $sqrt(5)$. The point count has here determined the trace, while the genus-one theorem supplied the shape and determinant of the numerator.]

In this example put $u_j=alpha^j+overline(alpha)^j$, with $alpha=-1+2i$. The quadratic equation gives
$ u_0=2, quad u_1=-2, quad u_(j+2)=-2u_(j+1)-5u_j. $
It follows that $u_2=-6$, $u_3=22$, and therefore $N_2=32$, $N_3=104$. Eight points were already defined over $𝔽_5$, so $frac(32-8,2)=12$ degree-two closed points account for the new points over $𝔽_25$. Likewise $frac(104-8,3)=32$ degree-three closed points account for the extension of degree three. The two complex numbers in the numerator control these exact integer counts over every extension. The square-root bound reads $|u_j|<=2 dot 5^(frac(j,2))$ and follows in this example immediately from the computed roots.

For a general smooth projective geometrically connected curve, the assertion $|alpha_i|=sqrt(q)$ is the profound ingredient. If it holds, a zero of $1-alpha_i q^(-s)$ satisfies $q^(Re s)=|alpha_i|$ and therefore $Re s=frac(1,2)$. The periodicity of $q^(-s)$ in the imaginary direction makes a finite set of inverse roots adequate for a whole vertical strip. The classical zeta function of the integers has infinitely many distinct zero ordinates without any such common period. The affine-line and elliptic-curve calculations show how spectral parameters enter prime counts. An exact formula for the sum allows one to estimate it by the sizes of those parameters. #link("https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9904-1949-09219-4")[Weil's 1949 account.]

== Multiplicity as an arithmetic resource <c02s07>

For a nonzero polynomial $F$ over a field, if distinct elements $a_1,dots,a_r$ are zeros of multiplicities at least $m$, then $m r<=op("deg")(F)$. Indeed the factors $(X-a_i)^m$ are pairwise coprime and their product divides $F$. On a smooth projective curve the corresponding statement uses the equality between the total degrees of the zeros and the poles of a nonzero rational function. If rational points are among the zeros, with imposed multiplicities, one obtains an upper bound for their number from the pole degree alone. To obtain a useful bound one must construct a function of moderate pole degree while forcing these zeros.

In characteristic $p$ an ordinary derivative can conceal a multiplicity. For $F(X)=X^p-a^p$, every first derivative vanishes identically. The coefficients in the expansion $F(X+U)=sum_j D_j F(X) U^j$ give a convenient replacement. At a given point $a$, the conditions $D_j F(a)=0$ for $0<=j<m$ are exactly divisibility by $(X-a)^m$. For the monomial $X^d$ one has $D_j X^d=binom(d,j)X^(d-j)$, with the binomial coefficient viewed in the ground field. These operators give linear conditions on coefficients of the auxiliary function. A dimension count can produce a nonzero solution if the number of free coefficients exceeds the rank of those conditions.

The raw count on the affine line explains what additional structure an effective argument needs. Vanishing $m$ times at each of the $q$ elements imposes $m q$ independent conditions on polynomials of degree less than $m q$, forcing the polynomial to vanish identically. Degree $m q$ first allows $(X^q-X)^m$. The elementary multiplicity inequality then returns only the known answer $q$. A useful curve argument chooses a space of functions in which the Frobenius relation couples the conditions at rational points, so the dimension and the pole costs balance more favorably. Alpöge's motivation invokes that gain from imposed multiplicity. In the number-field argument a period has an archimedean norm and finite-place divisibility, and a chain length replaces the single multiplicity $m$. The determinant comparison proving its gain is developed in the arithmetic chapters.

== The route taken in 2026 <c02s08>

In August 2026 Alpöge and Ralph Furman reported an unconditional lower proportion of $0.6725$ for zeros that are both simple and on the line. They credited Claude with finding the argument. Youness Lamzouri gave a different proof of this proportion the following month. The denominator in these proportions counts all nontrivial zeros with multiplicity. A sparse set of exceptions could coexist with either estimate. The November construction addresses the different question of how to detect even one off-line zero. #link("https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.13637v2")[Alpöge and Furman.] #link("https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02882v2")[Lamzouri.]

On 20 November 2026, Levent Alpöge announced that he and Claude had found a proof and released the Lean sources. He describes a transition from CM divisibility at inert primes to chains of theta lifts whose length is on the square-root scale. The five public conclusions concern ordinary zeta and Dirichlet $L$-functions, including imprimitive characters. The announcement also expresses an expectation about a further extension. The statements studied here are the five present in the release. #src("Main")[The five conclusions.]

There are now two threads to follow. The analytic thread asks how an off-line zero could be detected by a positive fluctuation of a prime sum. The arithmetic thread asks how a large fluctuation could violate an integral determinant inequality. The intervening chapters develop the transforms, finite characters, lattices, and cohomology needed to make those questions meet.

